WN.lb(41,{"mt_mRCPP_Ab2W":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Mammal babies are fed…","choices":["milk","only seaweed","lightning"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do mammal mothers provide?","explain":"Mammal babies drink milk from their mother."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Warm-blooded means…","choices":["the body stays warm inside","the animal matches icy water","the animal has no blood"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about body temperature in cold seas.","explain":"Warm-blooded animals keep a warm inside even in cold water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Fish often take oxygen using…","choices":["gills","blowholes only","milk"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which fish body part pulls oxygen from water?","explain":"Gills take oxygen from water. Blowholes belong to whales and dolphins."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A dolphin under water must…","choices":["come up for air later","grow gills in one minute","stop being a mammal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dolphins have lungs, so they cannot stay under forever.","explain":"Dolphins return to the surface for air. They do not grow gills or stop being mammals."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A list has {n} ocean animals. {k} are mammals. How many are not mammals?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the mammal count from the full list count.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} animals in the list are not mammals.","params":{"n":[3,6],"k":[1,3]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} whale calves each need milk. How many milk-feeds is that if each gets one?","answer":"n","hint":"Count one milk-feed for each calf.","explain":"Each calf needs one milk-feed, so {n} calves means {n} milk-feeds in total.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A blowhole is for…","choices":["breathing air","filtering mud only","laying eggs"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a whale do at the surface?","explain":"The blowhole lets the whale breathe air at the surface."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which list is all mammal clues?","choices":["air breath, milk, warm blood","gills, eggs in water, cold match","scales, fins only, no milk"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick the mammal trait list.","explain":"Air, milk and warm blood are mammal clues. The others fit fish better."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Whales and dolphins are…","choices":["mammals","fish","insects"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think air breath, milk and warm blood.","explain":"Whales and dolphins are mammals even though they live in the ocean."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A whale breathes…","choices":["air at the surface","only through gills","soil"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whales have lungs and a blowhole.","explain":"Whales come up to breathe air. Gills are a fish trait."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A class lists {n} mammal traits and {k} fish traits. How many mammal traits?","answer":"n","hint":"Count only the mammal traits.","explain":"They listed {n} mammal traits. The {k} fish traits are separate.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A mammal checklist has {n} traits. You already ticked {k}. How many still to tick?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the ones already ticked from the full checklist count.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} mammal traits still to tick.","params":{"n":[3,5],"k":[0,2]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Living in the ocean…","choices":["does not make an animal a fish","always means it is a fish","means it cannot be a mammal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Habitat alone does not decide the group.","explain":"Ocean homes do not force the fish label. Check mammal traits."}],"explain":{"kid":["Whales and dolphins live in the ocean, but they are mammals, not fish.","They breathe air at the top of the water. They are warm inside. Baby whales and dolphins drink milk.","Fish often have gills to take oxygen from water. Fish do not feed babies milk.","Living in water alone does not decide. Check breath, milk and warmth."],"adult":["Ages 5–7 science: mammals share traits (air-breathing lungs, milk for young, warm-blooded). Whales and dolphins are marine mammals. KS1 animals — group and compare. Words: mammal, fish, blowhole, gills, warm-blooded, extinct is not needed here.","Common mistakes: thinking anything in water is a fish; confusing gills with blowholes; thinking whales are cold like the sea; forgetting milk feeding as a mammal clue.","In the lab your child sorts mammal vs fish clues, brings a whale up to breathe, chooses milk for a baby dolphin, picks warm-blooded, rejects 'water = fish', and ticks the mammal checklist without gills."]},"familyTip":"At an aquarium or in a book, ask 'how does that whale breathe?' and 'is it a fish or a mammal — how do you know?'","keyIdea":"Whales and dolphins are mammals, not fish. They breathe air at the surface, are warm-blooded, and feed babies milk. Living in the ocean does not make an animal a fish — fish usually use gills and do not feed milk.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Mammal or fish? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Mammal clue","Fish clue"],"items":[["breathes air at the surface",0],["has gills",1],["feeds baby milk",0],["usually lays eggs in water",1],["warm-blooded",0],["takes oxygen from water with gills",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwhale","caption":"Flagship lab: sort mammal vs fish clues, surface-breathe, choose milk feeding, pick warm-blooded, reject water=fish, and tick the mammal checklist.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oAg79ju344":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A whale lives in…","choices":["the ocean","a sheep field","a toy box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whales are ocean animals.","explain":"Whales live in the ocean habitat, not fields or toy boxes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A swimming pool is…","choices":["not the ocean","the same as the ocean","saltier than every sea"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pools are man-made and small.","explain":"Pools are not oceans. Oceans are vast natural salt-water bodies."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Waves move…","choices":["on the ocean surface","only underground always","inside dry rocks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Watch the top of the water.","explain":"Waves move on the surface of the ocean."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why isn’t a puddle an ocean?","choices":["it is tiny and usually fresh","it has more whales","it is made of metal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare size and salt. Think about the ocean carefully.","explain":"Puddles are tiny and usually fresh. Oceans are huge and salty."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A poster shows {n} ocean animals. How many ocean animals?","answer":"n","hint":"Count the ocean animals on the poster.","explain":"The poster shows {n} ocean animals.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} seas are marked salty and {k} puddles are fresh. How many salty seas?","answer":"n","hint":"Count only the salty seas.","explain":"There are {n} salty seas marked.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The shore is…","choices":["where ocean meets land","a desert only","a metal wall in space"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think beach or coast. Think about the ocean carefully.","explain":"The shore is where the ocean meets the land."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which list is all about the ocean?","choices":["salt water, waves, deep water","bathtub, juice, sheep field","puddle, toy box, dry rock only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ocean traits: salt, size, habitat, waves.","explain":"Salt water, waves and deep water describe the ocean. Baths and fields do not."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The ocean is…","choices":["huge salt water on Earth","a garden puddle","always a bathtub"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the vast blue parts on a globe.","explain":"The ocean is a huge natural body of salt water. Puddles and baths are not oceans."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ocean water is…","choices":["salty","always fresh like a tap","made of juice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Seas and oceans taste of salt.","explain":"Ocean water is salty. Tap water and juice are not ocean water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A chart marks {n} shore signs and {k} deep-water signs. How many ocean-zone marks in all?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Add the shore marks and the deep-water marks.","explain":"{n} + {k} = {=n+k} ocean-zone marks in all.","params":{"n":[1,3],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} whale pictures and {k} puddle pictures. How many ocean-animal homes?","answer":"n","hint":"Count only the whale homes — whales live in the ocean.","explain":"{n} whale pictures are ocean homes. Puddles are not oceans.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Deep ocean water is…","choices":["farther from the shore","the same as shore sand","only in cups"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think shore → shallow → deep.","explain":"Deep water lies farther from the shore. Sand and cups are not the deep ocean."}],"explain":{"kid":["The ocean is huge salt water that covers most of Earth.","Near the land is the shore, then shallow water, then deep water farther out.","Ocean water is salty. Many ponds and puddles are fresh water.","Whales and many fish live in the ocean. A bathtub or swimming pool is not an ocean."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7 geography/science: ocean as a vast salt-water body and habitat; shore, shallow, deep; waves. Distinct from listing where water is found — here the focus is what the ocean is.","Common mistakes: calling every wet place an ocean; thinking ocean water is fresh; mixing pools/baths with oceans; saying whales live in fields; thinking the ocean is small.","In the lab your child builds zones, sorts ocean vs not, marks salt vs fresh, places a whale in the ocean, shows waves and vast size, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Look at a globe or map together. Point to the big blue parts and say 'ocean'. Ask: 'Is our bath an ocean? Why not?'","keyIdea":"The ocean is a huge body of salt water that covers most of Earth. It has a shore, shallow water and deep water. It is a habitat for sea animals. Puddles, baths and swimming pools are not oceans.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Ocean or not? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Ocean","Not the ocean"],"items":[["vast salt sea",0],["garden puddle",1],["open water with whales",0],["bathtub",1],["deep water far from shore",0],["swimming pool",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagocean","caption":"Flagship lab: build shore–shallow–deep, sort ocean vs not, mark salt water, and catch ocean myths.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_8QOeG3CuKc":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Southern Ocean wraps…","choices":["around Antarctica","only the Sahara","only city parks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look near the South Pole on many maps.","explain":"The Southern Ocean surrounds Antarctica."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Atlantic is…","choices":["a named ocean","a mountain range","a desert"],"answer":0,"hint":"It sits between the Americas, Europe, and Africa.","explain":"Atlantic is one of the five named oceans."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why five names?","choices":["regions of one global ocean","five sealed jars of water","five puddles only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Names help us talk about places on the map.","explain":"Five labels for connected salt-water regions."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Indian Ocean is…","choices":["one of the five oceans","only a river","only a cloud"],"answer":0,"hint":"It lies south of Asia on world maps.","explain":"Indian is a named ocean basin."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Pacific compared to Arctic…","choices":["Pacific is larger","Arctic is larger","same size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think largest versus smallest ocean.","explain":"Pacific is largest; Arctic is smallest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"True idea?","choices":["five names, one connected ocean","oceans never share water","oceans stop as sealed walls"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of continuous salt water on Earth.","explain":"Named regions share one global ocean."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"How many named oceans? Seed {k}. Enter 5.","answer":"5","hint":"Count Pacific through Arctic on a world map.","explain":"There are 5 named oceans (seed {k}).","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Arctic is 1 of how many named oceans? Seed {k}. Enter 5.","answer":"5","hint":"Arctic is one name in the full set of five.","explain":"Arctic is one of 5 named oceans (seed {k}).","params":{"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"order","id":"m9","prompt":"Order: from largest idea to coldest.","items":["name Pacific as largest","name the middle three oceans","name Arctic as coldest"],"hint":"Size first, then the rest, then coldest.","explain":"Pacific first, then the middle three, then Arctic."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"A pond is…","choices":["not one of the five oceans","the largest ocean","the Arctic Ocean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Oceans are vast salt-water basins.","explain":"Ponds and lakes are not the five oceans."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many named oceans?","choices":["Five","Two","Twenty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern, Arctic.","explain":"Maps use five ocean names on Earth."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Largest ocean?","choices":["Pacific","Arctic","a pond"],"answer":0,"hint":"It covers more area than the others.","explain":"The Pacific Ocean is the largest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Count the named oceans (there are five). Your seed is {k}. Enter 5.","answer":"5","hint":"Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern, Arctic — five names.","explain":"The answer is 5 named oceans (seed {k}).","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"How many ocean names on Earth? Seed {k}. Enter five as 5.","answer":"5","hint":"Say all five names, then write the count.","explain":"Earth has 5 named oceans (seed {k}).","params":{"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order: study the five oceans.","items":["find them on a world map","say each name aloud","note largest and coldest"],"hint":"Locate, name, then compare size and cold.","explain":"Map first, then names, then Pacific vs Arctic."}],"explain":{"kid":["Earth has five big oceans with names.","Their names are Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern, and Arctic.","Pacific is the biggest. Arctic is the smallest and the coldest.","The five names are parts of one big ocean that joins up."],"adult":["KS2 geography: five ocean names for one global ocean. Words: Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern, Arctic.","Common mistake: thinking walls seal oceans apart, or calling Arctic the biggest, or mixing oceans with ponds.","In the lab your child finds five spots on a map, picks Pacific as biggest and Arctic as coldest, and sees they join."]},"familyTip":"On a world map, point to each ocean by name. Ask which is biggest and which is near the North Pole.","keyIdea":"Earth has five named oceans — Pacific (largest), Atlantic, Indian, Southern, and Arctic (smallest and coldest) — that form one connected global ocean.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Ocean fact or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["Pacific is the largest ocean",0],["Arctic is smallest and coldest",0],["five names, one connected ocean",0],["five oceans sealed with walls",1],["Southern Ocean wraps Antarctica",0],["oceans are only city puddles",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagoce5","caption":"Flagship lab: map five oceans, name them, largest and coldest, connect, challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_m31_gPS8F1":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How does a dolphin get air?","choices":["it swims up and breathes through its blowhole","it uses gills like a fish","it does not need air"],"answer":0,"hint":"A dolphin has lungs like you, not gills.","explain":"Dolphins breathe air. They swim to the top and breathe through the blowhole on their head."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which part of the ocean suits a clownfish?","choices":["a warm, sunny reef","an icy polar sea","the deep, dark sea"],"answer":0,"hint":"A clownfish needs warm water and an anemone to hide in.","explain":"Clownfish live on warm, sunny coral reefs, where anemones grow. Icy or dark water does not suit them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A crab hides under a rock. Which need is it meeting?","choices":["a safe place, or shelter","food","air"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hiding keeps an animal away from hungry hunters.","explain":"Hiding under a rock gives the crab shelter, a safe place where big fish and birds cannot reach it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says sea animals only need sea water. What is wrong?","choices":["they also need food, a safe place and oxygen","sea animals need no water","nothing is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what a seal, a clownfish and a dolphin each need.","explain":"Sea animals need food, a safe place, oxygen and the right water, just like land animals need food, shelter and air."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} clownfish live in one anemone. {k} more come to hide. How many clownfish are in the anemone now?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Add the ones that came: {n} and {k} more.","explain":"{n} + {k} = {=n+k}. Now {=n+k} clownfish hide in the anemone.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How are a rabbit and a crab alike?","choices":["both need food, a safe place and air","they need different things","only the rabbit needs to hide"],"answer":0,"hint":"A rabbit hides in a burrow. Where does a crab hide?","explain":"Land and sea animals have the same needs. A rabbit hides in a burrow and a crab hides under a rock."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which animal needs icy water full of fish?","choices":["an emperor penguin","a clownfish","a starfish in a warm rock pool"],"answer":0,"hint":"This bird lives near Antarctica and dives to catch fish.","explain":"Emperor penguins live in the icy sea around Antarctica and catch fish there. A clownfish needs a warm reef."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A sea turtle eats {a} bites of seagrass, then {b} more. How many bites is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count on from {a} by {b}.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} bites of seagrass, a plant that grows on the sea floor.","params":{"a":[3,7],"b":[2,3]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does a seal need to eat?","choices":["fish","just sea water","sand"],"answer":0,"hint":"A seal is a hunter. It dives down to catch something that swims.","explain":"Seals catch and eat fish. Sea water is not food, and every animal needs food."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Why does a clownfish hide in a sea anemone?","choices":["to stay safe from big fish","to eat the anemone","to keep warm"],"answer":0,"hint":"The anemone has stinging arms. Big fish keep away from them.","explain":"The anemone is the clownfish's safe place, or shelter. Its stings keep big fish away, and slime on the clownfish stops it being stung."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A seal eats {a} fish in the morning and {b} fish later. How many fish does it eat in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Put the two groups together: {a} and {b} more.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The seal eats {=a+b} fish.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {n} crabs on the sand. {k} hide under a rock. How many crabs are still on the sand?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start with {n} and take away the {k} that hid.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k}. {=n-k} crabs are still out on the sand.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How does a fish get the oxygen it needs?","choices":["its gills take it from the water","it swims up to breathe air","fish do not need oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fish stay under the water all the time. What part of the body helps them?","explain":"Fish do need oxygen. Their gills take it out of the water, so they never need to come up for air."}],"explain":{"kid":["Sea animals need food, just like you. A seal eats fish. A sea turtle munches seagrass. A clownfish eats tiny bits of floating food called plankton. Sea water on its own is not food.","Sea animals need a safe place to hide, too. We call it shelter. A crab tucks itself under a rock. A clownfish hides in a sea anemone. The anemone stings big fish, but slime on the clownfish keeps it safe.","All animals need oxygen. It is a gas in the air and in the water. A fish gets oxygen from the water with its gills, so it stays under. A dolphin has no gills. It swims up to the top and breathes air.","The sea is not the same everywhere. A reef is warm and sunny. A polar sea is icy. The deep sea is dark. Each animal lives where the water suits it, just as a camel suits a desert."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. This fits the National Curriculum in England, Year 2 science, living things and their habitats: pupils describe how different habitats provide for the basic needs of different kinds of animals. It builds on Year 1 animals, including humans. Key words: basic needs, survive, habitat, shelter, oxygen, gills, adapted (suited).","Every animal needs food, water, oxygen and shelter, and a habitat that gives it the right conditions. In the ocean those conditions include temperature, light, depth and saltiness. A clownfish lives on warm, shallow coral reefs and eats zooplankton and algae. Its mucus coat stops its host anemone from stinging it, while the stings keep predators away. Fish take dissolved oxygen from water across their gills. Dolphins and whales are mammals with lungs, so they surface to breathe through a blowhole.","Common mistakes: thinking sea animals only need sea water; thinking fish do not need oxygen, or that they gulp air at the surface; thinking dolphins breathe with gills; believing all sea water is the same, so any sea animal could live anywhere; and thinking sea animals need different things from land animals, when only the way they meet the need changes.","In the lab your child drags food to a clownfish, a seal and a sea turtle. Next they drag a clownfish into an anemone and a crab under a rock before a big fish passes. Then they tap a fish's gills and drag a dolphin up to breathe. They place four animals in a warm reef, an icy polar sea, the deep sea or a rocky shore pool. Last, they sort six land and sea examples into food, a safe place and air."]},"familyTip":"At bath time or by a pond, pick one sea animal together, like a crab. Ask three questions: What does it eat? Where does it hide? How does it get air? Then ask the same three about your cat, dog or a garden bird. Spot that the needs match, even though the answers are different.","keyIdea":"Ocean animals need the same things as land animals: food, a safe place to hide (shelter), oxygen and the right conditions. A clownfish eats plankton and hides in an anemone. A fish takes oxygen from the water with its gills, but a dolphin swims up to breathe air. The ocean is not the same everywhere: a warm reef, an icy polar sea, the deep dark sea and a rocky shore pool each suit different animals.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it food, or a safe place to hide? Drag each card, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["food","a safe place"],"items":[["plankton for a clownfish",0],["an anemone for a clownfish",1],["seagrass for a sea turtle",0],["a gap under a rock for a crab",1],["fish for a seal",0],["an empty shell for a hermit crab",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagseaneed","caption":"Flagship lab: feed sea animals, hide a clownfish and a crab, help a fish and a dolphin get air, and find each animal the right part of the ocean.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4bJiGiMPmy":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Are all beaches sandy?","choices":["no, some coasts are rocky or muddy","yes, every beach is sand","yes, unless it is raining"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about rock pools and sticky mud.","explain":"Coasts can be sandy, rocky or muddy. Each kind looks and feels different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where would a curlew find its food?","choices":["in soft mud, using its long beak","on bare, hard rock","in a dry sand dune"],"answer":0,"hint":"A curlew has a very long, curved beak.","explain":"Curlews poke their long beaks into soft mud to find worms and shellfish."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which lives on a rocky coast?","choices":["a limpet","a lugworm","marram grass"],"answer":0,"hint":"It needs something hard to clamp onto.","explain":"Limpets clamp onto rocks. Lugworms and marram grass need sand."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Your boots sink into soft, sticky ground by the sea. What kind of coast is it?","choices":["a muddy coast","a rocky coast","a sandy beach"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hard rock does not squish. What does?","explain":"Soft, sticky ground you sink into is mud, so it is a muddy coast."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} curlews are on the mud. {k} more fly in. How many curlews now?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Count on from {n} by {k}.","explain":"{n} + {k} = {=n+k} curlews poking the mud for worms.","params":{"n":[2,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Tom says the edge of the village pond is a coast. Is he right?","choices":["no, a coast is where land meets the sea","yes, any water edge is a coast","yes, if the pond is big"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a pond part of the sea?","explain":"A pond or lake is water inside the land. Only the edge of the sea is a coast."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What holds the sand together on a sand dune?","choices":["the long roots of marram grass","seaweed","limpets"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a tall grass that grows on dunes.","explain":"Marram grass has long roots that hold the sand, which helps the dune stay in place."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A rock pool has {a} crabs and {b} starfish. How many animals is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the crabs and the starfish: {a} and {b} more.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} animals in the rock pool on the rocky coast.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is a coast?","choices":["the place where the land meets the sea","the edge of a pond or a lake","the middle of the sea"],"answer":0,"hint":"A coast always has two things side by side: land and the sea.","explain":"A coast is where the land meets the sea. The edge of a lake is not a coast, because a lake is not the sea."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which kind of coast has rock pools?","choices":["a rocky coast","a sandy beach","a muddy coast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rock pools are puddles of sea water left between hard rocks.","explain":"Rock pools form in hollows between rocks when the sea goes out, so you find them on rocky coasts."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You find {a} shells on the sand and {b} more by the rocks. How many shells in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Put the two groups together: {a} and {b} more.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. You found {=a+b} shells on the coast.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} limpets cling to a rock. {k} more limpets clamp on next to them. How many limpets are on the rock now?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Start with {n} limpets and count on {k} more.","explain":"{n} + {k} = {=n+k}. Limpets clamp onto rocks so the waves cannot wash them away.","params":{"n":[3,8],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Where does beach sand come from?","choices":["rock and shells broken into tiny bits by waves","it grows on the sea floor","it falls from the sky"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about waves crashing on rocks again and again for years.","explain":"Waves and weather slowly break rocks and shells into tiny bits. That takes a very long time."}],"explain":{"kid":["A coast is where the land meets the sea. A beach is the part of the coast you can walk on. The edge of a pond or a lake is not a coast, because a coast needs the sea.","Coasts are not all the same. A sandy beach has soft sand for digging. A rocky coast has hard rocks and rock pools. A muddy coast has soft, sticky mud that your boots sink into.","Each coast has its own plants and animals. Lugworms dig in sand, and marram grass grows on sand dunes. Limpets and seaweed hold on to rocks. Curlews poke their long beaks into mud to find worms.","Where does sand come from? Waves crash on rocks again and again. Over a very long time, rocks and shells break into tiny bits. Those bits are sand."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. This fits the National Curriculum in England, Key Stage 1 geography, which lists coast, beach, cliff and sea as key physical features, and Year 2 science, living things and their habitats. Key words: coast, coastline, beach, shore, sandy, rocky, muddy, habitat, rock pool.","A coast is the strip where land meets the sea. Sandy beaches build up where waves drop sand; rocky shores and cliffs form where waves wear hard rock; mudflats and salt marshes form in sheltered places such as estuaries, where fine mud settles. Most beach sand is weathered rock, often quartz, mixed with broken shell, ground down over very long times.","Common mistakes: thinking every beach is sandy; calling the edge of a lake a coast; thinking sand grows or is made quickly; and thinking the same animals live on every coast. Limpets need a hard surface to clamp onto, lugworms need sand to burrow in, and curlews need soft mud to probe.","In the lab your child drags a finger along the coastline on a map, while taps on a lake or the open sea get a short correction. Next they drag a hand lens over sandy, rocky and muddy coasts. Then they crash waves on a rock until it wears from boulders to pebbles to sand. Last, they find a coast for six living things and sort six clue cards."]},"familyTip":"Next time you see the sea, or a picture of it, play 'coast detective'. Ask: is the ground sand, rock or mud? What can you see living there? Look for squiggly lugworm piles on sand, limpets stuck on rocks, or birds with long beaks poking the mud.","keyIdea":"A coast is the place where the land meets the sea; a beach is the part of the coast you can walk on. Coasts are not all the same: some are sandy, some are rocky and some are muddy. Each kind has its own plants and animals, such as lugworms and marram grass on sand, limpets and seaweed on rocks, and curlews and samphire on mud. Beach sand is rock and shell worn into tiny bits by waves over a very long time.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sandy or rocky? Drag each card, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["sandy beach","rocky coast"],"items":[["build a sandcastle",0],["rock pools with crabs",1],["dunes with tall grass",0],["limpets stuck on hard rock",1],["squiggly lugworm piles",0],["seaweed hanging on boulders",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcoast","caption":"Flagship lab: trace the coast, look at sandy, rocky and muddy coasts, wear a rock into sand, and find each living thing its coast.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ytUG3yjCYt":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Is a sea star (starfish) a fish?","choices":["no, it has no fins and no gills like a fish","yes, because it is called starfish","yes, every sea animal is a fish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look past the name. Does it have fins?","explain":"A sea star is not a fish, even though some people call it a starfish."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which shape is a stingray?","choices":["flat, like a pancake","long and thin, like a rope","round and spiky"],"answer":0,"hint":"A stingray glides along the sea floor.","explain":"A stingray is flat. An eel is long and thin, and a puffed-up pufferfish is round and spiky."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How does a crab move?","choices":["it walks sideways on its legs","it squirts out a jet of water","it flaps flippers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the crab's legs and how it walks along the sand.","explain":"Crabs walk, often sideways, on their legs. An octopus uses a water jet, and a turtle flaps flippers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which ocean animal is about as long as your hand?","choices":["a clownfish","a blue whale","a great white shark"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think small: which one could sit on your open hand?","explain":"A clownfish is small, about as long as your hand. Whales and sharks are much bigger."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A reef has {a} clownfish and {b} turtles. How many kinds of animal is that?","answer":"2","hint":"Count kinds, not animals. How many different animals are there?","explain":"There are {=a+b} animals, but only 2 kinds: clownfish and turtles.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is a whale not a fish?","choices":["it breathes air and feeds its baby milk","it is too big to be a fish","it has no tail"],"answer":0,"hint":"How does a whale breathe?","explain":"A whale breathes air and feeds its baby milk, so it is a mammal, not a fish."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which animal has eight long arms?","choices":["an octopus","an eel","a sea star"],"answer":0,"hint":"It can squirt water to shoot along.","explain":"An octopus has eight arms. A sea star usually has five, and an eel has none."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A diver sees {n} sea stars. Each has 5 arms. How many arms is that?","answer":"5*n","hint":"Count on in fives, once for each sea star.","explain":"{n} × 5 = {=5*n} arms. Most sea stars have 5 arms each.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the biggest animal that has ever lived?","choices":["the blue whale","a dinosaur","the great white shark"],"answer":0,"hint":"It lives in the ocean and is longer than two buses.","explain":"The blue whale is the biggest animal ever. It is bigger than any dinosaur and much bigger than a shark."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Is a seahorse a fish?","choices":["yes, it has gills and fins","no, it is a kind of horse","no, it is a mammal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how a seahorse breathes under water.","explain":"A seahorse is a fish. It breathes with gills and swims with small fins."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A rock pool has {a} crabs and {b} shrimps. How many animals is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Put the two groups together: {a} and {b} more.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} animals, but only 2 kinds: crabs and shrimps.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A crab has 2 big claws. How many claws do {n} crabs have?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count in twos, once for each crab.","explain":"{n} × 2 = {=2*n}. Each crab has 2 claws, so {n} crabs have {=2*n} claws.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which animal is NOT a fish?","choices":["a jellyfish","a clownfish","a shark"],"answer":0,"hint":"A fish has fins and gills. Which one has neither?","explain":"A jellyfish is not a fish. It has no bones, no fins and no gills. Clownfish and sharks are fish."}],"explain":{"kid":["The ocean is home to lots of different animals. A clownfish is about as long as your hand. A blue whale is longer than two buses. It is the biggest animal that has ever lived, bigger than any dinosaur.","Sea animals come in many shapes. A sea star is shaped like a star. An eel is long and thin. A stingray is flat, like a pancake. A pufferfish can puff up into a spiky ball. An octopus has eight long arms.","Not every sea animal is a fish. A fish has gills to breathe under water and fins to swim. A seahorse is a fish! But a sea star, a jellyfish, a crab and a whale are not fish.","Sea animals move in different ways too. Fish swim with fins and a tail. Crabs walk sideways on their legs. An octopus squirts out water to shoot along. Turtles flap big flippers."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. This fits the National Curriculum in England, Year 1 science (identify and name a variety of common animals, including fish and mammals, and compare their structure) and Year 2 (living things and their habitats). Key words: variety, kind, fish, mammal, gills, fins, flippers, size, shape.","The blue whale grows to about 30 m and is the largest animal known to have lived. An adult great white shark is about 4 to 5 m, a green sea turtle shell about 1 m, and a clownfish about 10 cm. Seahorses are true fish, with gills, fins and a swim bladder. Sea stars and jellyfish are invertebrates without backbones. Crabs are crustaceans. Whales and dolphins are mammals.","Common mistakes: thinking every sea animal is a fish (so sea stars and jellyfish get called fish); thinking a seahorse is not a fish because of its name and shape; thinking a dinosaur or a shark was the biggest animal ever; and counting animals when asked for kinds. Three clownfish and one crab are four animals but only two kinds.","In the lab your child drags four animals onto a size line from smallest to biggest, matches five animals to body shapes, sorts seven into fish and not a fish, and matches four to how they move. Last, they tap every different kind on a reef: nine animals, but six kinds. Every action also has a button."]},"familyTip":"Look at a picture of a rock pool, a reef or a fish counter together. Count the animals, then count the kinds. Ask: which is the biggest? Which shape is strangest? Which ones are fish, and which are not?","keyIdea":"The ocean is home to a huge variety of animals. They come in every size, from a clownfish about as long as your hand to the blue whale, the biggest animal that has ever lived. They come in many shapes: star-shaped sea stars, long thin eels, flat rays, spiky pufferfish and octopuses with eight arms. Not all of them are fish. A seahorse is a fish, but a sea star, a jellyfish, a crab and a whale are not. Variety means how many different kinds there are.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fish or not a fish? Drag each card, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["fish","not a fish"],"items":[["clownfish",0],["seahorse",0],["shark",0],["sea star",1],["jellyfish",1],["whale",1],["crab",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagovar","caption":"Flagship lab: line ocean animals up by size, match body shapes, sort fish from not fish, match ways of moving and count the kinds on a reef.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sQpIV0-qY7":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which zone is dark all day and all night?","choices":["The midnight zone","The twilight zone","The sunlight zone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Its name sounds like the darkest time of night.","explain":"No sunlight reaches the midnight zone, so it is always dark there, even at midday."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Is the midnight zone dark at midday?","choices":["Yes, sunlight never reaches it","No, it is only dark at night","No, the Sun lights it at midday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Midnight is just its name. Think about how deep it is.","explain":"It is below 1000 m. Sunlight never gets that deep, so it is dark all the time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where does most sea life live?","choices":["In the sunlight zone","In the midnight zone","The same amount in every zone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do the plants grow?","explain":"Plants grow in the sunlight zone, and lots of animals eat them, so most sea life is near the top."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"An anglerfish has a glowing lure to catch food in the dark. Which zone is it from?","choices":["Midnight zone","Sunlight zone","A rock pool"],"answer":0,"hint":"A glowing light only helps where it is dark.","explain":"Its lure works in total darkness, so it lives deep in the midnight zone."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Nothing grows in the midnight zone. What do animals there eat?","choices":["Bits of food that sink down from above","Plants that grow in the dark","They do not eat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happened when you dropped food in the lab.","explain":"Bits of dead plants and animals sink from the sunlit zone. This 'marine snow' feeds the deep, and some deep animals eat each other."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A diver is at {=d*10} m. The twilight zone starts at 200 m. How many more metres down is the twilight zone?","answer":"200-d*10","hint":"Take the diver's depth away from 200.","explain":"200 − {=d*10} = {=200-d*10}. The twilight zone starts {=200-d*10} m further down.","params":{"d":[2,18]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the twilight zone like?","choices":["Dim, with too little light for plants","Bright, with lots of seaweed","Totally dark, with no light at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twilight is the time of evening when it is getting dark.","explain":"Some dim blue light reaches it, but not enough for plants to make food."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A sea turtle has to swim up to breathe air. Which zone does it live in?","choices":["Sunlight zone","Twilight zone","Midnight zone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Air is at the top of the sea.","explain":"It stays near the surface to breathe and eats sea grass and jellyfish there, in the sunlight zone."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"The twilight zone starts at 200 m. A lanternfish is resting {=d*100} m deep. How many metres below the top of the twilight zone is it?","answer":"d*100-200","hint":"Take 200, where the twilight zone starts, away from the fish's depth.","explain":"{=d*100} − 200 = {=d*100-200}. The lanternfish is {=d*100-200} m below the top of the twilight zone.","params":{"d":[3,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What happens to sunlight as you go deeper into the sea?","choices":["It fades until there is none","It stays the same","It gets brighter near the sea floor"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the light meter on the submarine.","explain":"Water soaks up sunlight. By 200 m almost all of it is gone, and below 1000 m there is none."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The midnight zone starts at 1000 m. A submarine is at {=d*100} m. How many more metres must it dive to reach the midnight zone?","answer":"1000-d*100","hint":"Take the depth it is at away from 1000.","explain":"1000 − {=d*100} = {=1000-d*100}. It must dive {=1000-d*100} m more.","params":{"d":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which list puts the zones in order from the top to the bottom?","choices":["Sunlight, twilight, midnight","Midnight, twilight, sunlight","Twilight, sunlight, midnight"],"answer":0,"hint":"The top gets the most light.","explain":"From most light to least: sunlight on top, twilight in the middle, midnight at the bottom."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A fish swims {=s*10} m below the surface. The sunlight zone goes down to 200 m. How many metres deeper could the fish go and still be in the sunlight zone?","answer":"200-s*10","hint":"Take the fish's depth away from 200.","explain":"200 − {=s*10} = {=200-s*10}. It can go {=200-s*10} m deeper before it reaches the twilight zone.","params":{"s":[1,19]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why do sea plants only grow in the sunlight zone?","choices":["They need light to make their food","The water is too cold for them lower down","Fish eat all the plants lower down"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the sunlight zone have that the others do not?","explain":"Plants use light to make food. Below 200 m there is too little light for them."}],"explain":{"kid":["Sunlight shines down into the sea. But water soaks it up. The deeper you go, the less light there is. Far down, there is no light at all.","So the sea has three zones. The sunlight zone is the top part, down to 200 m. It is bright. The twilight zone goes from 200 m to 1000 m. It is dim, like the sky in the evening. The midnight zone is below 1000 m. It is dark all day and all night.","Sea plants need light to make their food. So they only grow in the sunlight zone. Lots of animals eat the plants, so most sea life lives near the top.","Deep animals still find food. Bits of dead plants and animals sink down from above, like snow. Some deep fish, like the anglerfish, make their own light to catch prey."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. Ocean scientists divide the open ocean into layers by how much sunlight reaches them: the sunlight (epipelagic) zone from the surface to about 200 m, the twilight (mesopelagic) zone from 200 m to 1000 m, and the midnight (bathypelagic) zone below 1000 m. Deeper layers exist, but these three are the ones children meet first.","Light drops quickly because water absorbs it. In clear water only about 1% of surface light is left at 200 m, which is too little for plants to make food. Below about 1000 m no sunlight is left at all. The only light there is made by animals themselves (bioluminescence).","Common mistakes: thinking light stays the same all the way down; thinking the midnight zone is dark only at night; putting plants in every zone; and thinking nothing can live where nothing grows. Deep animals eat 'marine snow', bits of dead plants and animals and droppings that sink from above, or they eat each other.","In the lab your child dives a submarine to 1000 m while a light meter falls to zero. They name the zones in order and plant sea plants that only grow near the top. They put a turtle, a lanternfish and an anglerfish in their zones, then drop food that sinks down to the anglerfish."]},"familyTip":"Fill a tall glass or a clear bottle with water and add a few drops of milk. Shine a torch down from the top in a dark room and look at the side: the light is bright at the top and gets dimmer lower down. Ask which part would be the sunlight, twilight and midnight zone.","keyIdea":"Sunlight fades as it goes down into the sea, so the open ocean has three zones. The sunlight zone is the top 200 m, where there is plenty of light. The twilight zone, from 200 m to 1000 m, has only dim light. The midnight zone, below 1000 m, is dark all the time. Plants need light to make food, so they only grow in the sunlight zone, and that is where most sea life lives.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which zone is it? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["☀️ Sunlight zone","🌆 Twilight zone","🌑 Midnight zone"],"items":[["Seaweed and tiny plants grow here",0],["Sea turtles swim up here to breathe",0],["Dim blue light, too little for plants",1],["Lanternfish hide here in the day",1],["No sunlight at all, day or night",2],["Anglerfish hunt with a glowing lure",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdepth","caption":"Flagship lab: dive a submarine and watch the light fade, name the three zones, plant sea plants, find each animal its home, and drop food down to the deep.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_kCSy3Lsgme":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What causes the tides?","choices":["Mostly the Moon's gravity","The wind","The heat of the Sun"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, what did you move to make a high tide?","explain":"The Moon pulls on the sea and makes it bulge. The Sun's pull adds a little, but the Moon does most of it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"About how many high tides does a beach usually get in one day?","choices":["2","1","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"There are two bulges of water, and the Earth spins once a day.","explain":"The beach passes through the bulge facing the Moon and the bulge on the far side, so it gets about 2 high tides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which wind makes the biggest waves?","choices":["A strong wind that blows for a long time","A gentle wind for a short time","No wind at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about blowing gently and blowing hard in the lab.","explain":"The stronger the wind and the longer it blows, the bigger the waves it pushes up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What does a wave carry across the sea?","choices":["Energy","The same water all the way","Sand from the beach"],"answer":0,"hint":"The duck stayed in the same place.","explain":"The water goes round and up and down and stays about where it was. What moves along is the wave's energy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The Gulf Stream is a current that carries warm water to cold places. What can that do?","choices":["Make the weather on nearby land milder","Make the tides stop","Make the Moon pull harder"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the warm water bring with it?","explain":"Warm water warms the air above it, so lands near a warm current have milder winters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A current carries a bottle {=k*10} km each day. How far does it carry the bottle in 10 days?","answer":"k*100","hint":"Multiply the distance for one day by 10.","explain":"{=k*10} × 10 = {=k*100}. The bottle travels {=k*100} km.","params":{"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"At low tide, where is the sea?","choices":["Further out, so more beach shows","Higher up the beach than ever","Gone from the beach for the whole day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Low means the sea level is low.","explain":"At low tide the sea has gone out, so more sand and rock pools show. A few hours later it comes back in."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The sea at a harbour is {=h} m deep at high tide. At low tide it is {=f} m less. How deep is it at low tide?","answer":"h-f","hint":"Take the fall away from the high tide depth.","explain":"{=h} − {=f} = {=h-f}. At low tide it is {=h-f} m deep.","params":{"h":[8,12],"f":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which one is NOT made by the wind?","choices":["The tide coming in","Waves crashing on the beach","Ripples on a pond"],"answer":0,"hint":"One of these is caused by the Moon.","explain":"Waves and ripples are pushed up by wind. The tide is the Moon's gravity pulling the sea."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What makes most of the waves on the sea?","choices":["Wind blowing over the water","The Moon pulling on the sea","Big fish swimming"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what you did to make waves in the lab.","explain":"Wind pushes the top of the water into waves. Stronger wind makes bigger waves."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Most beaches get 2 high tides each day. How many high tides does a beach get in {=d} days?","answer":"2*d","hint":"Each day has 2 high tides, so count up in 2s for each day.","explain":"2 high tides each day for {=d} days is 2 × {=d} = {=2*d} high tides.","params":{"d":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A wave passes under a floating ball. What does the ball do?","choices":["It bobs up and down in about the same place","It rides along with the wave to the beach","It sinks under the water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember the duck in the lab.","explain":"A wave carries energy, not water. The ball goes up and down as the wave goes by, then stays put."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"At low tide the water by a rock is {=a} cm deep. The tide comes in and the water rises by {=b} cm. How deep is it at high tide?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the rise to the low tide depth.","explain":"{=a} + {=b} = {=a+b}. At high tide the water is {=a+b} cm deep.","params":{"a":[10,40],"b":[50,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What is an ocean current?","choices":["A huge flow of water moving through the sea, like a river","A very big wave","The sea coming up the beach"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the bottle in the lab.","explain":"A current is a steady flow of water that can travel right across the ocean. Waves and tides are different."}],"explain":{"kid":["Wind makes waves. When wind blows over the sea, it pushes the top of the water up into bumps. A strong wind that blows for a long time makes big waves.","A wave moves across the sea, but the water does not go with it. A duck on the sea just goes up and down as each wave goes by. The wave carries energy, not water.","Tides are the sea coming up the beach and going back out. The Moon pulls on the sea with its gravity. The water piles up in a bulge on the side facing the Moon, and there is a bulge on the far side too. As the Earth spins, a beach goes through both bulges. So most beaches get two high tides and two low tides each day.","Currents are like huge rivers in the sea. They flow for thousands of miles. Some carry warm water from hot places to cold places, and cold water back. They also carry food for sea life."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. Three kinds of water movement often get mixed up. Waves are surface disturbances raised by wind; their size depends on wind speed, how long it blows and the stretch of open water it blows over. In a wave the water moves round in small circles and ends up roughly where it started, so a wave carries energy, not water.","Tides are the rise and fall of sea level caused by the gravity of the Moon and, to a lesser degree, the Sun. The Moon's pull raises one bulge of water on the side of the Earth facing it and a second on the far side. As the Earth rotates, most coasts pass through both bulges, giving two high and two low tides about every 24 hours and 50 minutes, so high tides are about 12 hours 25 minutes apart. Some places get only one tide a day; the lab shows the common pattern and keeps the Moon still.","Ocean currents are continuous flows of water. Surface currents are driven mainly by wind and shaped by the spinning Earth and the coasts; deep currents are driven by differences in temperature and saltiness. The Gulf Stream carries warm water from the tropics across the Atlantic and helps keep north-west Europe mild. Currents also move nutrients that feed plankton.","Common mistakes: thinking the Moon makes waves or the wind makes tides; thinking a wave carries water along with it; expecting one high tide a day; and calling a current a big wave. In the lab your child blows wind to grow waves, watches a duck bob as waves pass, drags the Moon to move the bulge, spins the Earth through a day and counts two high tides, then follows a bottle round a current."]},"familyTip":"Float a cork or a plastic bottle lid in a bath or a washing-up bowl. Blow across the water through a straw to make waves, and watch the cork bob up and down without travelling. If you visit a beach, put a stick in the sand at the water's edge and come back two hours later: has the sea come in or gone out?","keyIdea":"The sea moves in three different ways. Waves are made by wind blowing over the water; a wave carries energy along, but the water itself mostly stays in place. Tides are the slow rise and fall of the sea level, about twice a day, caused mainly by the Moon's gravity pulling on the sea. Currents are huge flows of water, like rivers in the ocean, that carry warm and cold water and food for sea life around the world.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a wave, a tide or a current? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["🌊 Waves","🌙 Tides","➡️ Currents"],"items":[["Made by wind blowing on the water",0],["A floating duck just bobs up and down",0],["The sea comes up the beach and goes out again, about twice a day",1],["Caused mostly by the Moon's pull",1],["Like a river flowing through the ocean",2],["Carries warm water to cold places",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtide","caption":"Flagship lab: blow up waves, watch a duck bob, pull the sea with the Moon, count a day of tides, follow a current.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
